 The angles at the vertices of a convex octagon sum to $1080^{\circ}$, so a convex octagon with the greatest number of interior right angles would have $ 1080-90-n $ interior angles that make up $360+n$ degrees, where $n$ is the number of right angles.

If $n$ is too high, then these angles will sum to more than $900º$, which is impossible because the entire angle sum is 1080. If we try $n=7$, then these angles will sum exactly to $360+7=367^º$, so a convex octagon can have $\boxed{7}$ right angles.

Note: It is possible to construct a convex octagon with 7 right angles. If 7 of the vertices of the octagon are close together, and the remaining vertex cones out the right angle, then the octagon is convex. [asy]
